{{also|capulet}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Surname of the heroine Juliet's family in Shakespeare's ''Romeo and Juliet'', from the {{der|en|it|Capuleti}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A member or citizen of the family, party, or country of the wife in a [[Romeo and Juliet couple]] and/or one of a pair of feuding groups, the other identified as [[Montague]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913| title=Dirk, a South African |author=Annabella Bruce Marchand | page=198| passage=It goes without saying that she knew nothing whatever of the bad relations subsisting between her father and her sweetheart. She did not know her Romeo was a Montague still less that to him she was a '''Capulet'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963| title=George C. Marshall: Education of a general, 1880-1939 |author=Forrest C. Pogue | page=8| passage=Still, despite Marshall's impression of a kind of Montague-'''Capulet''' feud, George Catlett did succeed in marrying Laura Emily, the daughter of Dr. Jonathan Bradford, and his sister, Margaret, married Laura's brother, Thomas.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004| title=Cardinal Sin: Tales of Alaska, War, and More |author=Scott Casper | page=161| passage=She playfully called herself a tragedienne, much like Juliet '''Capulet''', and she thought of Timmy as her Romeo Montague. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=The Knowledge Machine|author=Michael Strevens | page=| passage=That said, '''Capulet's''' skepticism has a sound rationale. She needs only one kind of thing, caloric fluid, to explain the movement and behavior of heat. Montague has posited two kinds of things, heat itself and heat radiation, each popping up to do the job it is good for and then conveniently metamorphosing into the other when that's what's needed instead.}}

